Title = {Origin, metal deposition mechanisms and short wavelength infrared (SWIR)
   spectroscopy study at the Yinzishan epithermal Au deposit, Yunnan
   Province, SW China},
Journal = {ORE GEOLOGY REVIEWS},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {170},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {The Yinzishan epithermal Au deposit discovered in the Simao block, is
   dominated by telluride minerals, and contains a total of >= 20 t of Au
   with ore grading at 8.90 g/t Au. The mineralization is hosted within
   thick accumulations of steeply dipping andesitic to rhyodacitic
   volcaniclastic units. The distribution of Au orebodies in this deposit
   is largely controlled by NNW-oriented faults and the higher porosity and
   permeability of the volcaniclastic host rocks. A detailed study of the
   Yinzishan deposit geology, occurrence of gold, and short wavelength
   infrared (SWIR) data from the northern to southern sections of the
   deposit was performed to characterize the hydrothermal minerals and
   alteration zonation, and assess the deposition mechanisms of the metal.
   The dominant country-rock alteration of the deposit is silicification,
   advanced argillic alteration, chloritization, sericitization, and
   pyritization and the main ore mineral combination is
   pyrite-enargite-covellite-chalcopyrite-calaverite. Au mainly occurs as
   native gold and calaverite or micronsized inclusions in tellurides
   (e.g., melonite and tetradymite) and pyrite. The characteristics of the
   ore minerals and alteration assemblages indicate that the ore-forming
   fluid was relatively oxidized, with SO2-, H2S-, and F-rich acidic
   hydrothermal fluids. This feature supports the highsulfidation origins
   of the Yinzishan Au deposit. Tellurium was introduced into the site of
   ore deposition via vapor in F-rich fluid and HS-complexes are thought to
   have been the main carriers of Au. The mixing of magmatic fluids with
   meteoric waters was the crucial control on the precipitation of the Au
   telluride ore. The SWIR spectroscopy revealed that the mineral
   combination of topaz + alunite has a good coupling relationship with Au
   mineralization. Moreover, the SWIR scalars of four characteristic single
   minerals/mineral assemblages, including topaz (Dep2086 > 0.2), alunite
   (Dep1470 > 0.06), chlorite (Pos2250 > 2250 nm), and kandite and/or
   pyrophyllite (Dep2170 > 0.07), provide a visualization tool for
   determining the ore-forming environment and the location of
   hydrothermal/mineralization centers. Therefore, we proposed that the
   combined application of detailed SWIR-based analyses of alteration
   minerals and interpretations of the SWIR scalars of characteristic
   minerals is an effective approach for the exploration of epithermal
   hydrothermal deposits.},
